{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Actors and Actions in the Business Diagram  "}, {"type": "position", "content": 1}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 2}, {"type": "level", "content": "A1.1"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you hear to talk about business process"}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Understands systems theories, business processes and basic concepts on IT.  Create a map of processes that allows identifying areas in an information system, using IT tools to generate reports based on the company's needs."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Understands simple information in slow conversations about the communicative topics and vocabulary related to this level.  Recognizes basic ideas on IT concepts, systems theory, and diagrams of processes."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe8n1le02ob01re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Prepositions of Place  "}, {"type": "describer", "content": "Prepositions of place are words that you use to express the position of a thing, person, animal or abstraction."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The use of these prepositions evidences the relation in space between people, place, and things.</p> <p>The prepositions of place normally answer the questions <strong><i>where?,</i></strong> and they go before a noun.</p> <p>Let&rsquo;s have a look at the following prepositions:</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p><strong>Above</strong></p> <p>You use <strong><i>above</i></strong> when you want to talk about an object that is in a higher position than your point of reference.</p> <p><i>Example: </i></p> <p><i>The business books you are looking for are <strong>above</strong> those documents. (The point of reference is a pile of documents, and the business books are in a higher position.)</i></p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p><strong>Under</strong></p> <p>You use <strong><i>under</i></strong> when you want to talk about an object that is placed in a lower position than your point of reference. Usually, the point of reference covers the object.</p> <p><i>Example:</i></p> <p><i>The business books she is looking for are <strong>under</strong> my bed. (The point of reference is the bed, which covers the books.)</i></p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p><strong>Across</strong></p> <p>You use<strong> <i>across </i></strong>when you refer to something that is placed or moves from one side to the other side of your point of reference. Usually, this point of reference is a piece of land, a river or a road. It can also refer to an object that is on the opposite side of your point of reference. &nbsp;</p> <p><i>Example:</i></p> <p><i>I walk <strong>across</strong> the river to get home. (The river is my point of reference. I walk from one side of the river to the other side of it.)</i></p> <p><i>The library where I buy the business books is <strong>across </strong>the road. (The road is my point of reference and the library is on the opposite side of it.)</i></p> <p>Other prepositions of place are:</p> <ul> <li>In front of</li> <li>Behind</li> <li>Above</li> <li>Under</li> <li>Below</li> <li>Next to</li> </ul>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Place the actors above the lines, and under each of them, write down their actions. "}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": " Imperatives "}, {"type": "describer", "content": "Imperatives are verbs that we use to give commands, instructions, and make suggestions or invitations. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Draw the diagram at the top of your pages. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Place the actors above the lines, and under each of them, write down their actions."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<p>You form <strong>the imperative</strong> by using <strong><i>the infinitive</i></strong> of the verb without <strong><i>to</i></strong>.</p> <p>Example:</p> <p><strong><i>Write</i></strong><i> that report now!</i></p> <p><strong><i>Come</i></strong><i> here and <strong>read</strong> this for me!</i></p> <p><strong><i>Bring </i></strong><i>that book.</i></p> <p>Follow this structure to form imperative sentences. Notice that in these type of sentences there is no subject (you, she, he etc.).</p> <p>Affirmative Imperative: <strong><i>Send </i></strong><i>me the report before 9 am.</i></p> <p>Negative Imperative:<strong> <i>Don&rsquo;t stay</i></strong><i> up late.</i></p> <p>Look at some of the contexts you can use infinitives:</p> <ul> <li>Direct order: <strong><i>Go</i></strong><i> there</i>.</li> <li>Give instructions: <strong><i>Listen</i></strong><i> to the audio.</i></li> <li>Invitation: <strong><i>Come</i></strong><i> in and <strong>sit</strong> down</i>.</li> <li>Make suggestion: <strong><i>Don&rsquo;t go</i></strong><i>, it will rain.</i></li> </ul>"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "involve"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to include something or somebody to be part or a process, situation or circumstance"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Think about a procedure that involves ordering, producing, and selling a product with a swim lane flowchart. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n1le02ob01aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "remember"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to have  in  mind or recall something"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: First, you have to remember that all businesses have processes."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n1le02ob01aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "step"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a series of levels or stages that someone follows to do something "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: There are people and steps involved in them, and there is also a system that we use to get things done. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n1le02ob01aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "billing"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a document that is the proof of a purchase or order"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: For example, there are production, billing, and human resources processes, among others. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n1le02ob01aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "sell"}, {"type": "definition", "content": " to negotiate with goods or services for a price that was agreed "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Think about a procedure that involves ordering, producing, and selling a product with a swim lane flowchart.   "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n1le02ob01aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "rectangle"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a geometric figure that has four lines, two parallels sides, one larger than the other"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Put each one in a rectangle."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n1le02ob01aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Think about a business process. Search information about its typical steps."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Design a flowchart to explain the necessary steps to complete the process."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Present the flowchart to your class."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Attend the recommendations and make the necessary changes."}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"